Subject: [PATCH 1/4] rebase: adopt upstream #412-#418; fix upstream
 VecSubI32x8 width-test failure; port still-needed local work
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Red-team audit outcome over the five newest upstream PRs (#414-#418)
plus the two that carried our prior work (#412, #413).

Upstream defect fixed (the reported CI failure):
- src/ir/intrinsics.rs: the width unit test listed the four x8 lane ops
  (VecSubI32x8, VecAndI32x8, VecOrI32x8, VecXorI32x8) under the 128-bit
  'narrow' list asserting Some(16), while vector_result_width correctly
  reports Some(32) (8 lanes x 4B; codegen emits vpsubd/vpand/vpor/vpxor
  on %ymm via emit_avx_binary_256 — verified). The implementation was
  right; the test data was the defect. The test is now EXHAUSTIVE BY
  CONSTRUCTION: it extracts every Vec* variant from this file's enum via
  include_str! at compile time and derives the expected width from the
  name's last <Type>x<Lanes> token (documenting the Horizontal /
  VecStore / unlowered-NEON-shape divergences), so a future op added to
  the enum is checked automatically instead of relying on a hand-pasted
  list — the exact failure mode that produced this bug.

Ported from the validated local branch (still-needed upstream):
- stack_layout/copy_coalescing.rs, memfold_consumer_256: order-correct
  memory-operand fold gates for the integer map lane ops (And/Or/Xor
  commutative, Sub src2-only, matching the packed min/max contract).
  Measured A/B on current upstream: without the port the xor kernel
  emits vmovdqu loads plus stack round-trips; with it the consumer folds
  as vpxor (mem), %ymm, %ymm — fewer loads, no spill traffic.
  is_vec_ssa_producer gains the same four families for list consistency
  with every other value-producing Vec* family. On today's upstream the
  missing entries are correctness-NEUTRAL (the deferred-store path was
  reorganized by #413/#416; the d[i]=a[i]-b[i] segfault the comment
  warned about does not reproduce at -O2/-O3, v3/SSE2, stack or static
  arrays, or under CCC_NO_SMALL_SLOTS) — kept as hardening against the
  next reader who trusts the classification, with the stale claim
  removed.
- backend/i686/codegen/peephole.rs: whole-function GPR liveness oracle
  (backward dataflow, per-function extents, return-uses-edx) replacing
  fold_memory_operands' barrier-assumed deadness; register-allocated
  values (maxi(a,b){return a>b?a:b;} param home) are no longer folded
  across labels/conditional jumps/back-edges. Spot-verified: maxi keeps
  its param homes across the select; divmod's cltd/idivl implicit
  edx:eax handling survives every rename.
- tests: vex_promote_semantic.c (VEX promotion semantics: remainder
  tails, jump-table dispatch, live-128-bit-half soundness, self-zeroing
  idioms, conversions) and the richer vectorize_int_map_lanes.c
  (0..17 size sweep, I64x2 lanes, invariant broadcasts in both operand
  orders, all four strict min/max folds + two non-strict no-folds, NaN/
  +-0 pairings, in-place aliasing kernel).

Deliberately NOT ported (upstream is strictly better, data-checked):
- vectorize.rs non-IV-phi guards stay in analyze_map_pattern and
  analyze_stencil_pattern where upstream put them. Hoisting the guard
  into analyze_loop_pattern (our local revision) is a LATENT MISCOMPILE:
  analyze_loop_pattern is the matmul analyzer, not a shared entry, and
  its None falls through to analyzers without the guard — reproduced
  locally as k_acc(1)=0 (want 3) on vectorize_carried_value_and_negzero
  before reverting. Upstream's placement is correct.
- generation.rs cast-peel (#415), i686 divrem assert (#412), live_range
  cost model (#412), if-convert deref coverage (#413), C23 va_start
  (#413), slot-root widening (#416), epilogue cross-jumping (#418):
  audited sound and strictly stronger than the local state.

Verification: cargo test --lib 1956/1956; regression suite 626 PASS /
0 FAIL / 15 SKIP (ELF32: no runner on this host); map_sub emits vpsubd,
4x4 matmul emits 16x vfmadd, i64 reduction emits paddq, xor map folds
vpxor with a memory operand.
